LZ4 between two haproxy instances: measurements and thoughts
============================================================

Idea (2026-11-08): implement LZ4 as an alternate output format. Browsers
don't accept it, so it would only work between two haproxy instances
(e.g. across a WAN or between datacenters), with a custom content-coding
configured on both sides. LZ4 is normally much faster than the deflate
formats, especially to decompress. See ../encoding/README for the
measurement tools and ../brotli/README for the comparison with brotli.


1. The format
-------------

LZ4 is LZ77 without any entropy coding, everything is byte-aligned. The
block format is a series of sequences:

  token (1 byte: 4 bits literal count, 4 bits match length - 4)
  [literal count extension: bytes of 255, then a last byte < 255,
   present when the 4-bit count is 15]
  literals (raw bytes)
  offset (2 bytes, little endian, 1-65535)
  [match length extension, same scheme, when the 4-bit length is 15]

Constraints: minimum match 4; the last 5 bytes of a block are always
literals; the last match must start at least 12 bytes before the end of
the block; the last sequence has only the token and the literals.

The frame format wraps blocks: magic number, frame descriptor (flags,
block max size 64 kB to 4 MB, optional content size), then blocks each
preceded by a 4-byte size whose high bit marks an uncompressed block,
an end mark, and optional xxHash32 checksums (per block and/or content).
Blocks can be declared independent, which fits slz's stateless calls.

What this means for an slz-style encoder:
  - slz already defers literals until a match is found (<plit>), so at
    match time both the literal count and the match length are known:
    emitting a token, the extensions, a memcpy of the literals and the
    offset is trivial, and there is no bit queue at all;
  - no 9-bit literals, hence no stored blocks, no debt/credit logic and
    no cost tests: incompressible data costs one extension byte per 255
    literals plus a token, and a block can always be sent uncompressed
    for 4 bytes;
  - slz's window (32 kB) is within LZ4's 64 kB offset limit, and LZ4
    matches are not capped at 258;
  - the end-of-block constraints need a little care in the main loop.


2. What an LZ4 emitter fed by slz's match finder would produce
--------------------------------------------------------------

SLZ_STATS (patch 0001 in this directory, originally commit 84989ac;
it applies on top of ../encoding/0001-0003 and ../brotli/0001) computes the size of slz's own parse in LZ4
block format (tokens, extensions, offsets, literals, 5 bytes per call).
The parse is still decided for deflate (cost tests, 258 cap), so it is
approximate. Against the real lz4 1.10.0 on the same blocks:

                 slz -1 parse  slz -4 parse  lz4 1.10.0
  hap-org  4 kB     50.61%        47.93%       47.95%
  hap-org 16 kB     42.43%        39.72%       39.69%
  hap-org 32 kB     40.24%        37.16%       37.07%
  hap-org  1 MB     34.24%        34.14%       33.26%
  silesia  4 kB     61.76%        58.06%       57.90%
  silesia 16 kB     56.88%        52.34%       52.12%
  silesia 32 kB     55.02%        50.23%       49.93%
  silesia  1 MB     51.09%        47.52%       47.68%

Average sequence overhead: 3.10-3.17 bytes per reference.

So slz's level 4 match finder is as good as lz4's own for LZ4 output (it
is the same design: 4-byte hash, single entry, greedy parsing). Level 1
loses 3-5 points to its literal skip.

Speed: SLZ_DRY_RUN=7 (nothing encoded, ../encoding/README section 2) gives the
speed of slz's search and decisions alone: 557.8 MB/s at -4 -k 32 on
silesia and 725.6 at -1, vs 608 MB/s for lz4 (different CPU core and
harness, see section 4). An LZ4 emitter adds back a memcpy and a few
bytes per sequence, but removes the deflate-specific decisions, so slz
with an LZ4 emitter should land at roughly 85-90% of lz4's compression
speed at the same ratio with level 4 (the search alone is at 92%), or
faster than lz4 at a worse ratio with level 1. Not implemented, so not
measured.


3. LZ4 against the deflate formats (lzbench + zenc + zdec, 2026-11-08)
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Each block compressed independently. Ratio, compression MB/s,
decompression MB/s. lz4/lz4hc/libdeflate/zlib/zstd from lzbench; slz
from the current zenc (compression) and zdec (decompression, i.e. uslz),
since lzbench's slz is the old 1.4-dev3. Xeon W-2145, taskset -c 4.

  hap-org: ratio / compression MB/s / decompression MB/s
                    4 kB                  16 kB                 32 kB                 1 MB                  
  lz4                47.95%  632  3152     39.69%  669  3155     37.07%  682  3146     33.26%  731  3758    
  lz4 fast-3         52.86%  750  3320     42.73%  737  3293     39.33%  741  3276     36.05%  801  3791    
  lz4 fast-17        78.27% 1753  5199     62.15% 1373  4088     55.54% 1235  3796     49.74% 1214  4227    
