  libdeflate -1      42.37%  161   582     38.14%  217   797     36.71%  227   896     34.75%  232  1030    
  libdeflate -6      40.67%   92   580     35.98%  109   799     34.27%  106   889     31.93%   99  1064    
  zlib -1            43.15%   74   252     39.29%  101   317     38.05%  108   341     36.51%  102   368    
  zstd -1            43.19%  288   786     38.17%  365  1035     37.22%  421  1206     34.72%  469  1452    
  slz -1 (current)   57.08%  553   673     52.54%  595   668     50.87%  606   674     47.07%  555   624    
  slz -4 (current)   52.62%  416   541     47.41%  445   551     45.57%  455   565     43.16%  448   537    
  slz -T (current)   53.01%  376   403     47.15%  404   412     44.89%  417   420     41.75%  421   393    

Observations:
  - lz4 compresses only 0-30% faster than slz -1 on this machine (608-
    731 MB/s vs 533-623, the gap being largest at 4 kB and 1 MB), and
    lz4's --fast modes buy speed with ratio much like slz's literal skip
    does;
  - the big difference is decompression: 2.8-3.8 GB/s for lz4 vs 0.4-
    0.67 GB/s for uslz, i.e. 4-6x against slz -1 streams and up to 9x
    against -T ones (dynamic block). That is the real argument for LZ4
    between two haproxy instances: the receiving side's CPU;
  - the price is the ratio: at 32 kB lz4 is between slz -1 and -4 on
    silesia (49.93% vs 50.87%/45.57%), similar to slz -1 on the HTML
    (37.07% vs 36.06%) but 33% larger than slz -T there (27.96%). Over
    a WAN where bandwidth is the reason for compressing at all, that may
    matter more than the CPU;
  - lz4hc reaches slz -4 ratios on the HTML (lz4hc -4: 31.31% at 32 kB)
    with the same decompression speed but at 137 MB/s to compress;
  - for reference, zstd -1 beats slz -T's ratio on the HTML (26.11% vs
    27.96% at 32 kB) at 443 MB/s and decompresses at 1.36 GB/s; its per-
    stream memory was not measured, and that's what matters for haproxy.
    Browsers accept zstd as a content-coding nowadays, as far as I know,
    unlike lz4.


4. Conclusions (2026-11-08)
---------------------------

  - No real gain to expect on the compression side. slz's level 4 search
    already finds what lz4 finds (same size in LZ4 format, section 2),
    and lz4 compresses only 0-30% faster than slz -1. An LZ4 emitter on
    slz's search would be simple to write (slz already holds literals
    back until a match, so the literal count and match length are known
    when the token is due; no bit queue, no stored blocks, no debt logic,
    4 bytes for an uncompressed block; only the end-of-block rules need
    care), and would reach about 85-90% of lz4's speed at the same ratio.
  - The only real argument is decompression, 4-9x faster than uslz,
    i.e. CPU saved on the receiving haproxy. It comes at the price of a
    much larger output than slz -T on text (+33% on the HTML at 32 kB),
    which matters if bandwidth is why we compress between the two sites.
  - zstd -1 would be the interesting one (better ratio than slz -T, 1.36
    GB/s to decompress, accepted by browsers), but it is way more complex
    to implement: unlike LZ4 or brotli it is no longer a matter of output
    format (FSE/huffman entropy stages, sequence coding). Its per-stream
    memory was not measured.
  - Observation that also supports the matcher's design: several modern
    implementations copy slz's design (single-entry 4-byte hash, greedy
    parsing), and lz4 lands at exactly the same parse quality.


5. Commands used
----------------

lzbench (commit 32ab077: lz4 1.10.0, libdeflate 1.26, zlib 1.3.2, zstd
1.5.7), -b<kB> compresses each block independently, -t1,1 sets the
minimum time of each measurement to 1 second, -o4 prints CSV
(compressor, compression MB/s, decompression MB/s, original size,
compressed size, ratio, file):

  cd lzbench
  for f in /tmp/hap-org.html /dev/shm/silesia.tar; do
      for b in 4 16 32 1024; do
          taskset -c 4 ./lzbench -b$b -t1,1 -o4 \
              -elz4/lz4fast,3,17/lz4hc,1,4,9/slz_deflate,1,2/libdeflate,1,6/zlib,1/zstd,1 $f
      done
  done

zenc and zdec builds (zenc as in ../brotli/README section 7, zdec the way the
decoder is usually measured, without the jump padding):

  gcc-15 $F -o zenc src/zenc.c src/slz_common.c src/slz.c
  gcc-14 -O3 -g -fomit-frame-pointer -march=native -mno-avx512f -Isrc \
      -o zdec src/zdec.c src/slz_common.c src/uslz.c

slz compression, best of 3 (-l 200 on the HTML, -l 3 on silesia):

  taskset -c 4 ./zenc <-1|-4|-4 -T> -k <kB> -l 3 -t -v silesia.tar

slz decompression: raw deflate streams produced with the same options,
then decoded by zdec, best of 3, zdec's own MB/s (decoded bytes over the
timed rounds):

  ./zenc <opts> -k <kB> -D -c silesia.tar > s.raw
  taskset -c 4 ./zdec -D -l 3 -t -v s.raw

LZ4 estimate of slz's parse (the "lz4 estimate" line of SLZ_STATS):

  make zenc USR_CFLAGS="-DSLZ_STATS=1"
  ./zenc -4 -k 32 -t hap-org.html 2>&1 | grep "lz4 estimate"
